FORCE_SSL_PROMPT:NO accepts certificates that fail verification.

lynx.cfg documents NO as: questionable conditions "will be reported,
but not attempted". In an OpenSSL build the only place a verification
failure is judged is HTSSLCallback(), and it tests

    if (!(preverify_ok || ssl_okay || ssl_noprompt))

-- ssl_noprompt for truth. FORCE_PROMPT_YES and FORCE_PROMPT_NO are both
non-zero, so NO skips the block exactly as YES does, the callback
returns 1, and the connection proceeds. HTForcedPrompt(), which the
block would have called, answers NO for FORCE_PROMPT_NO and so would
have refused. (The GnuTLS path, HTCheckCertificate's tail, compares
against FORCE_PROMPT_NO explicitly and is correct.)

Measured with Lynx 2.9.3 as a distribution builds it, against a
self-signed certificate for the right name: default settings refuse
("self-signed certificate ... Unable to make secure connection");
FORCE_SSL_PROMPT:NO fetches the page.

Skip the block only for FORCE_PROMPT_YES.

--- a/WWW/Library/Implementation/HTTLS.c	2026-09-27 12:35:33.221756856 -0600
+++ b/WWW/Library/Implementation/HTTLS.c	2026-09-27 12:35:33.236084556 -0600
@@ -178,7 +178,11 @@
     FREE(msg);
 #endif
 
-    if (!(preverify_ok || ssl_okay || ssl_noprompt)) {
+    /* Skip the check only when told to say YES. FORCE_PROMPT_NO is
+     * non-zero too, and testing ssl_noprompt for truth let it skip the
+     * check as well -- accepting the very certificates it exists to
+     * refuse. HTForcedPrompt() answers NO for it below.  */
+    if (!(preverify_ok || ssl_okay || ssl_noprompt == FORCE_PROMPT_YES)) {
 #ifdef USE_X509_SUPPORT
 	SSL_single_prompt(&msg,
 			  X509_verify_cert_error_string((long)
